#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Curated original prototype prose. Content is illustrative and not research-verified."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
# Article array: canonical id, kicker, strapline, chapter-like reading blocks (title, narrative paragraphs).
ARTICLES={
'I.1.1':('THE DEEP LAND','Before any kingdom, language or border, there was the making of the land itself.',[
('A landscape before the story',[
'To begin a history of Korea with a king would be to arrive extraordinarily late. Long before cities, kingdoms, written records, or even the earliest human camps, the land that would become the Korean Peninsula was shaped through processes working on timescales almost beyond imagination. Mountains were raised and eroded, buried rock was transformed under pressure, rivers cut their passages, and shorelines moved as the earth and the sea changed around them. This is the deep setting for everything that follows.',
'The peninsula is not a uniform block of land. Its ridges and basins, its contrasting eastern and western coasts, and its intricate relationship with continental northeast Asia created different possibilities for human settlement. In later centuries the same landforms would influence the direction of roads, the locations of capitals, the movement of armies, and the growth of farming communities. Geography never dictated history by itself, but it established a remarkably durable set of opportunities and constraints.',
'Geological history must also be approached with a different kind of evidence. There are no eyewitnesses and no chronicles for the formation of an ancient mountain belt. Instead, scientists read rock layers, minerals, faults, sediments, and the structures preserved underground. Each is part of a record created without writing. Reading that record is an act of reconstruction: sometimes precise, sometimes provisional, and frequently revised as new measurements become available.'
]),
('The long conversation of stone',[
'The Korean Peninsula contains rocks with very different origins and ages. Some record the remains of ancient continental crust; others were produced as sediments accumulated, buried materials metamorphosed, or molten rock cooled into solid masses. This variety tells a story not of one dramatic birth but of repeated episodes of formation and transformation. A landscape that appears continuous to a traveler may be a mosaic of geological histories stitched together over immense spans of time.',
'Tectonic forces help explain why rock bodies are folded, fractured, intruded, or tilted. Erosion then reshapes the result. A mountain range is not simply something that rose once and remained unchanged. Its surface is continuously affected by rain, frost, rivers, vegetation, slope movement, and changes in climate. Even when geological motion becomes comparatively quiet, the landscape continues to evolve. Valleys widen, sediment moves downstream, and coastlines receive the material worn away from distant highlands.',
'These processes created the underlying materials of later human life. Stone supplied raw material for tools, bedrock helped determine relief, and weathering contributed to soils with different characteristics. Rivers connected uplands to lowlands. The history of land therefore flows into the history of resources, livelihoods, settlement, and technology, although the human uses of these environments came vastly later.'
]),
('Ridges, basins and the shape of movement',[
'People often imagine a country first as an outline on a map. But an outline reveals little of what movement inside that space feels like. A deep mountain valley may connect communities along its length while making journeys across neighboring ridges difficult. A river basin can gather farms, markets, and settlements around a shared water system. Open lowlands create opportunities for cultivation and movement that differ from those of steep, forested terrain.',
'In historical Korea, eastern mountain systems, western and southern lowlands, river corridors, and coastal approaches would repeatedly influence patterns of political consolidation. No single geographical feature explains the formation of a kingdom or the outcome of a war. Technology, institutions, population, and decisions all mattered. Yet rulers and ordinary households alike operated in a terrain that offered some routes more readily than others.',
'The best way to carry geography into a long historical narrative is to keep returning to it rather than treating it as an introductory diagram. A valley that first appears as a natural landform may reappear as a settlement corridor, a military passage, a market region, or a remembered cultural landscape. The same place can acquire new significance without losing the physical structure beneath it.'
]),
('Reading what survives',[
'The visible surface of the peninsula is only the latest chapter of a much longer sequence. Older landscapes have been buried or destroyed. Sediments preserve incomplete evidence of former environments, while rock formations record processes that may not have any direct modern equivalent. Investigators therefore build explanations from fragmentary traces and from comparison with processes observable elsewhere.',
'This produces an important distinction between a historical scene and a geological reconstruction. We can describe how erosion works and identify the materials present in a mountain, but we should not pretend to know exactly how every valley looked at each moment in deep time. A responsible narrative can still be vivid: it can explain processes, scale, consequence, and uncertainty without converting possibility into invented observation.',
'From here the journey leads into rivers, coasts, climate, ecosystems, and ultimately the arrival and lives of human populations. The landscape is not the preface to Korean history in the sense of being disposable background. It is a continuing participant in the narrative, changing slowly while human societies transform much faster.'
])]),
'II.1.2':('THE ORIGINS WE TELL','A foundation story opens a window onto memory, identity and the difficulty of beginnings.',[
('Where history meets the story of origins',[
'Among the most enduring narratives associated with the beginnings of Korea is the story of Dangun. It is commonly retold as an origin account for Gojoseon, but its significance extends beyond any straightforward question of whether a particular founding event happened in the form described. The story matters because communities have returned to it repeatedly to explain antiquity, belonging, legitimacy, and the relationship between the human world and a sacred or extraordinary past.',
'A foundation narrative is not the same object as a contemporary administrative record. Stories travel through time. They are shaped by transmission, preservation, selection, and reinterpretation. A text written down centuries after the period it describes can be an excellent source for understanding the beliefs and cultural memory of the time when it circulated, while still being an uncertain guide to the much earlier events it appears to recount.',
'This distinction does not make the tale historically unimportant. On the contrary, it makes possible several different histories at once: the history of the story, the history of its preservation, the history of changing interpretations, and the separate archaeological problem of reconstructing early societies on the peninsula and in neighboring regions.'
]),
('A world of transformation',[
'In a familiar version of the Dangun tradition, a heavenly figure and a human world become connected through events involving a bear and a tiger. Transformation, endurance, descent, and the establishment of an earthly order provide the narrative with themes that reach beyond a royal genealogy. The tale links political beginnings to a cosmos alive with agency and meaning.',
'It would be misleading to read every element as literal reportage. The changing animal figures are part of a transmitted mythological account, not independently verified archaeological facts. Yet they allow us to ask how later audiences imagined the relationship between nature, moral testing, social order, and leadership. Different retellings and historical settings may emphasize different aspects of that relationship.',
'Origin stories are often powerful precisely because they connect many scales. They locate one people or polity inside a larger moral universe, give the landscape symbolic depth, and make collective time feel continuous. Such continuity can be politically useful and emotionally compelling even when the evidence for a precise ancient foundation date is limited.'
]),
('The archaeological problem',[
'When archaeological researchers investigate the world from which early Korean polities emerged, they study settlements, burials, ceramics, bronze objects, exchange networks, and changes in social differentiation. These are material questions. They can reveal the emergence of hierarchy, intensified production, regional interaction, and changing forms of authority, but they do not automatically identify the named characters of later mythic narratives.',
'The temptation to draw a direct line from a particular artifact to a particular figure in a story should therefore be resisted. Similar objects may circulate across communities with different identities. A burial style does not uniquely encode ethnicity; a bronze implement does not reveal the words spoken by its owner. Archaeology is enormously informative, but its evidence answers some questions more readily than others.',
'Gojoseon itself raises genuine historical questions about chronology, location, and the interpretation of early written references. These questions deserve investigation on their own terms. The Dangun tradition belongs beside this inquiry as historical memory, rather than being made to do the evidentiary work of excavation or contemporary documentation.'
]),
('A story that continues to live',[
'Across later centuries, the Dangun narrative could be recalled in very different political and cultural environments. Its presence in memory reflects not one unchanging interpretation but repeated acts of selection and use. An origin can become a symbol of continuity under external pressure, a subject of national storytelling, a point of scholarly argument, or a source of ritual and commemorative meaning.',
'This makes the history of reception as important as the narrative itself. Who quoted the story? In which period? For what audience? Against which other accounts of the past? When such questions are asked, the myth becomes part of a dynamic history of how people imagined Korea, not merely a proposition to label true or false.',
'The natural detour from this topic is into the early states, the material world of bronze and iron, and the ways retrospective historical writing shaped the origins of later kingdoms. The tale of Dangun is one entrance into the vast space between remembered beginnings and recoverable history.'
])]),
'III.1.5':('A KINGDOM EXPANDS','The age of Gwanggaeto reveals how power, campaigning and memory reshaped northeast Asia.',[
('Northern horizons',[
'Goguryeo emerged as one of the major political powers of ancient northeast Asia. Its history cannot be understood solely within the boundaries of the modern Korean Peninsula, because its centers, campaigns, rivalries, and networks extended across regions that are now divided among different states. This broader setting is essential to the reign of Gwanggaeto, remembered as a ruler associated with unusually ambitious expansion and military initiative.',
'The end of the fourth and beginning of the fifth century were not a period of stillness around Goguryeo. Political authority was contested among different states and regional powers, and military force interacted constantly with diplomacy, alliance, mobility, and claims of legitimacy. A ruler who sought territorial influence had to deal not only with armies but with strongholds, logistical routes, subordinate groups, and neighboring courts.',
'The story of expansion is therefore more complex than a row of arrows on a map. Military events changed relationships among communities and political elites. Victories could strengthen royal authority, but control over frontier regions had to be maintained through continuing institutions and negotiations.'
]),
('Campaigns and the making of power',[
'Gwanggaeto is associated with campaigning in multiple directions and with the extension of Goguryeo influence over neighboring powers. The political meaning of such campaigning cannot always be reduced to modern notions of permanent annexation. Ancient rulers might assert dominance through tribute, military intervention, political dependency, the control of important places, or claims expressed in monumental inscriptions.',
'Territorial maps are useful as introductory aids, but they are especially dangerous when they portray a sharp, continuous border for periods when authority could be layered and fluctuating. Some regions experienced direct administration, while others may have been related through alliances or unequal diplomatic relationships. Understanding what influence meant at a particular place and time is a research question, not an automatic consequence of a successful battle.',
'A long narrative of this reign should give room to roads, river valleys, forts, military households, local communities, and the consequences of campaigning for ordinary inhabitants. The sources are uneven, making reconstruction necessary. That limitation should encourage thoughtful explanation rather than a false impression that every journey and conversation is known.'
]),
('The inscription and the image of a ruler',[
'One of the most important sources for Gwanggaeto and the royal ideology surrounding him is the monumental stele associated with his reign and erected in the following reign. Monumental inscriptions are not neutral chronicles. They are public acts of political memory: selected achievements are recorded in forms intended to communicate authority and shape the interpretation of the past.',
'The inscription is precious because it provides an early written perspective on Goguryeo history. Yet its wording, interpretation, and the politics of later readings demand care. Scholars have debated how to understand passages concerning Goguryeo and other regional powers, particularly where ancient Korea–Japan relations are at stake. The existence of an inscription does not remove the need to ask whose viewpoint it represents.',
'Its physical form matters as well. A monumental text is encountered as stone, labor, location, language, and public display. The act of preserving a ruler in such material form links political history to the history of writing, artistic production, dynastic remembrance, and changing modern interpretations.'
]),
('After the victories',[
'Royal achievements became part of a longer Goguryeo story, including the development of central authority and a changing strategic focus under later rulers. The achievements of one reign did not suspend political change. Rival kingdoms adjusted, opportunities shifted, and frontier relationships required renewed attention.',
'Gwanggaeto can be explored through several narrative paths: military organization, the political geography of northeast Asia, court institutions, inter-polity diplomacy, inscriptional culture, and the modern memory of ancient kingdoms. The same reign is many histories at once, each opened by a different question.',
'For readers following the larger chronology, the natural continuation is the reign of Jangsu and Goguryeo’s changing political orientation. For those taking a detour, the history of Baekje or early diplomacy leads to a different angle on the same world.'
])]),
'VI.4.2':('THE MAKING OF A SCRIPT','A new alphabet transformed the possibilities of recording Korean speech.',[
('A language and its written forms',[
'In fifteenth-century Joseon, Korean was a spoken language with a rich social life, but the main prestige writing system used in state administration and elite scholarship was Classical Chinese. Koreans had also developed methods for representing Korean elements through adaptations of Chinese characters. These practices were important and sophisticated, yet they did not provide the kind of straightforward, broadly usable representation of Korean sounds later associated with Hangul.',
'The difficulty was not that people lacked language, thought, or cultural expression before a new script. Rather, writing operated within institutions, education systems, and conventions that placed certain demands on readers and writers. The relationship between spoken Korean and high-status written culture shaped administration, learning, and who could participate in particular forms of textual exchange.',
'A new script could therefore be more than a technical innovation. It could make language itself the subject of careful analysis and open additional possibilities for documenting speech. But such possibilities depended on political sponsorship, social reception, teaching, and the availability of texts.'
]),
('Sejong and the intellectual setting',[
'King Sejong’s reign is remembered for broad interests in governance, learning, measurement, language, and knowledge production. The development of Hunminjeongeum belongs within this world of royal intellectual projects. The new script was not simply an accidental collection of signs; it was accompanied by an explicit explanation of its principles and its intended ability to represent Korean speech.',
'The historical record distinguishes the creation of Hunminjeongeum in 1443 from its promulgation in 1446. These dates help anchor the narrative, but the deeper question is what sort of problem the script was intended to solve and why its design took the form it did. Answering requires attention to phonology, the conventions of learned writing, and the courtly setting in which the project was developed.',
'An inviting history of the script can explain the underlying concepts without requiring readers to know linguistic terminology in advance. Consonants and vowels are a practical starting point. More specialized detours can examine articulatory features, syllable-block organization, and the relationship between the script and contemporary theories of sound.'
]),
('Letters as a designed system',[
'Hangul is notable for its systematic design. The shapes and combinations of its letters are often explained in relation to how speech sounds are produced and classified. A reader encountering the script for the first time may immediately notice that the written syllable is assembled as a block rather than set out simply as an unbroken row of isolated letters. This gives the page a visual order that is both linguistic and graphic.',
'Any simple account must be careful not to project the modern form of Korean writing unchanged into the fifteenth century. Some letter forms and sound distinctions used in early texts differ from ordinary modern practice, and orthographic conventions evolved. The story of Hangul is also the story of how a designed writing system entered living use and changed with society.',
'There is a wider context to such a development: writing technologies are never only alphabets or signs. They involve paper, printing, education, administrative priorities, literary patronage, and readership. The invention of a script creates a possibility; the gradual development of written culture determines how widely that possibility is used.'
]),
('From court innovation to public memory',[
'The later history of Hangul was neither immediate universal acceptance nor a single uninterrupted triumph. Classical Chinese retained great prestige in many elite contexts, while vernacular writing expanded across different genres and audiences over time. The changing balance between scripts was linked to education, gender, literature, administration, and the wider social world.',
'Under modern colonial rule, language and script became bound to questions of cultural survival and identity. In contemporary Korea, Hangul carries immense public symbolic significance, though modern everyday use is shaped by national division, regional variation, technological change, and standardization. These later developments belong to their own historical units rather than being collapsed into the invention story.',
'The invention of a writing system is an ideal example of the larger KOREA reading experience. A reader can begin with a single question—why did Sejong commission a script?—and follow paths into phonetics, royal government, print culture, women’s letters, colonial language politics, and digital Korean today.'
])]),
'VIII.9.2':('THE MARCH OF A NATION','Public demonstrations in 1919 reveal a broad and complex struggle against colonial rule.',[
('A colony and a moment of mobilization',[
'In 1919, demonstrations demanding Korean independence spread under Japanese colonial rule. The March First Movement is remembered for the public expression of national aspirations, but its importance lies also in the breadth of participation, the networks through which ideas and news circulated, and the variety of local experiences that formed the movement.',
'The colonial setting matters. Annexation in 1910 had fundamentally changed the political order, and administrative and police systems shaped daily life. Korean responses ranged across accommodation, survival, cultural organization, religious and educational activity, political dissent, and more explicitly revolutionary projects. The movement did not emerge from an empty political landscape.',
'Events beyond the peninsula influenced the political language of the period. Global conversations about self-determination, the aftermath of world war, and changing international orders offered terms in which aspirations might be expressed, even when outside powers were unwilling or unable to deliver the result protesters hoped for.'
]),
('Networks of public action',[
'Accounts of the movement frequently begin with declarations and prominent organizers, but collective action depended on far wider communication. Religious communities, students, teachers, local activists, traders, and ordinary residents carried news, assembled, printed or passed along texts, and interpreted events in their own settings. What looked like one nationwide event was also many locally organized moments.',
'In towns, markets, and villages, gatherings could take different forms. Some were planned through existing networks; others developed as news of demonstrations traveled. The pace and scale of participation varied across localities. The geography of communication—roads, schools, religious networks, and markets—became part of the history of resistance.',
'Women and young people were not merely spectators. Their participation complicates narratives organized only around prominent male leaders. Histories focused on local witnesses can reveal courage, constraints, disagreement, family consequences, and different forms of public involvement that broad political summaries may overlook.'
]),
('Repression, suffering and testimony',[
'Japanese colonial authorities responded to demonstrations with coercion and repression. The experiences of arrest, violence, imprisonment, and intimidation shaped how participants and observers remembered the events. Precise numbers and descriptions require careful attention to the sources that produced them, because colonial statistics, nationalist accounts, foreign reports, and later testimony emerged from different positions.',
'A history attentive to civilians can move beyond the confrontation between a colonial government and a national movement. It can ask how households managed fear, how communities responded to detained neighbors, and how stories of protest circulated afterward. The cost of political mobilization extended into ordinary life.',
'Historical writing need not invent dialogue to convey these realities. The structures of policing, the public nature of demonstrations, and the consequences of participation provide enough material for vivid explanation when handled with appropriate distinctions between documented events and general context.'
]),
('A movement and its many afterlives',[
'The March First Movement did not immediately end colonial rule. Its consequences instead unfolded through changes in political organization, diasporic activism, protest memory, colonial governance, and the formation of new strategies for independence. The Provisional Government of the Republic of Korea emerged in the same historical moment, while many other movements pursued distinct ideological and organizational routes.',
'Later generations made March First central to accounts of national resistance. Commemorations, schools, monuments, research, and political discourse have continually reshaped its public significance. This is one reason to keep the event and its memory analytically distinct: one concerns events in 1919, and the other concerns what subsequent people made of them.',
'This subject opens outward to questions of colonial schooling, religion, international activism, imprisonment, the press, political ideology, and daily life under Japanese rule. No one article needs to contain everything. A connected reader can follow each path without losing the larger chronology.'
])]),
'IX.1.1':('A SUDDEN OPENING','August 1945 was both an end of colonial rule and the beginning of an uncertain political future.',[
('The end of one order',[
'In August 1945, the collapse of Japanese imperial rule transformed the political possibilities of the Korean Peninsula. The end of colonial authority did not simply bring an immediate, settled national government into existence. Institutions had been shaped by decades of imperial administration, while Korean organizations and individuals held sharply different ideas about legitimate authority and the direction of reconstruction.',
'Liberation was therefore simultaneously a moment of hope and a practical crisis of power. Who could issue orders, maintain public services, organize policing, distribute resources, and speak for the country? These were not abstract constitutional questions. They shaped how towns, workplaces, villages, and households encountered the transition.',
'Historical memory often frames the date as a clear break between two periods, and in important respects it was. But some structures, personnel, inequalities, and immediate shortages carried across that boundary. Understanding both rupture and continuity is necessary for explaining why the months after liberation proved so politically consequential.'
]),
('The politics of an uncertain moment',[
'Korean political organizations attempted to shape the emerging order through committees, negotiations, declarations, and local organizing. The Committee for the Preparation of Korean Independence and the associated efforts to establish new authority belong to the rapidly evolving story of August and September 1945. Political legitimacy was contested from the beginning.',
'Ideological differences mattered, but they were not the only divisions. Leaders and communities disagreed about institutional procedure, property, social reform, local authority, political representation, and the relationship between Korean initiative and foreign power. Individuals sometimes changed positions as the situation changed.',
'Local organization could be especially important where central institutions were weak or uncertain. A nationwide narrative must leave room for these regional variations rather than imagining that political developments in a capital automatically determined what happened everywhere else.'
]),
('Division enters the frame',[
'International decisions and military developments placed Korea within the postwar settlement among major powers. Soviet and American occupation zones emerged around the 38th parallel, creating a new political geography that profoundly affected competing projects for a unified Korean state.',
'The line did not instantly explain every later development. Division hardened through institutional choices, negotiations, conflict, external intervention, and the formation of distinct political orders. Treating all subsequent events as inevitable would obscure the alternatives and uncertainties that participants actually confronted.',
'It is useful to follow the chronology slowly. The liberation moment, the establishment of occupation zones, the growth of competing authorities, and the failure to construct an accepted unified settlement are related but distinct developments. Each deserves its own place in an extensive historical narrative.'
]),
('Ordinary life inside the transition',[
'Changes in sovereignty are experienced through daily systems: food distribution, workplaces, transport, schools, policing, return migration, and access to information. Liberation did not suspend these needs. People had to continue living while authority was disputed and institutions were reorganized.',
'The movement of former soldiers, workers, exiles, and refugees added to the social complexity of the period. Expectations about land, employment, justice, and the treatment of people linked to colonial power differed across communities. Questions of collaboration and survival could not always be answered by simple moral categories.',
'The story continues naturally into the occupation administrations and the founding of rival states. A reader can also move sideways into personal memory, local committees, international diplomacy, or the broader history of postwar decolonization in Asia.'
])]),
'X.5.10':('CULTURE ACROSS BORDERS','How global networks helped transform Korean popular culture into an international phenomenon.',[
('Cultural movement, not a single event',[
'Hallyu, frequently rendered as the Korean Wave, refers to the international circulation and popularity of South Korean cultural forms. Television dramas, film, music, games, fashion, food, and online media have participated in this development, though their audiences, histories, and distribution networks are not identical.',
'It is tempting to describe the phenomenon as the inevitable result of a few successful entertainers or a unified national strategy. A more revealing account looks at shifting media markets, production companies, broadcasting systems, audience practices, digital platforms, and the often unpredictable ways a cultural work finds people far from where it was made.',
'Global reach also changes the work itself. Producers may consider international audiences, translation and subtitling practices shape reception, and fans create explanations, communities, performances, and new media around the original material. Circulation is never simply a one-way export.'
]),
('From broadcasting to platforms',[
'Before the contemporary dominance of streaming and social networks, television broadcasting, recorded media, regional distribution, and fan communities provided important routes for Korean entertainment beyond national borders. Different parts of Asia became significant spaces of circulation and reception, with particular programs and performers gaining traction through channels that changed over time.',
'Digital networks later transformed speed and scale. A performance could circulate through videos, excerpts, shared commentary, and user-created materials. Streaming platforms enabled some audiences to discover Korean-language content without first encountering it through domestic television schedules or traditional import channels.',
'This transformation produced new kinds of intermediaries. Translators, online communities, recommendation systems, critics, influencers, and independent fans could all affect visibility. Technology supplied channels, but actual cultural attachment came from people choosing to listen, watch, discuss, and return.'
]),
('Fans become participants',[
'Global fandom is not merely a measurement of attention. Fans organize communities, circulate translations, debate meanings, create derivative art, attend events, buy products, and sometimes engage in public advocacy. These activities can strengthen the durability of a cultural phenomenon after the release of any particular work.',
'At the same time, fan communities are diverse. Their experiences differ by language, country, age, economic resources, and access to platforms. International popularity does not erase inequalities in visibility or distribution. A global chart position and a local community gathering describe different dimensions of the same broader system.',
'Cultural exchange also runs in multiple directions. International audiences reinterpret Korean works through their own experiences, while Korean producers encounter foreign trends and collaborative possibilities. The history of Hallyu is thus part of a larger history of globalization, rather than proof of a self-contained cultural origin.'
]),
('Policy, markets and argument',[
'Government cultural policy, copyright systems, commercial investment, and the economics of entertainment production all influence the environment in which Hallyu develops. Yet cultural success cannot be reduced to policy alone. Creative decisions, labor conditions, management practices, distribution agreements, and audience agency all participate in the outcome.',
'International attention brings controversies as well as opportunities. Questions about representation, cultural translation, working conditions, ownership, privacy, and the demands placed on performers become more visible as industries grow. These are part of the history of cultural power, not side notes external to it.',
'The contemporary nature of this topic requires regular updating. What seems like the definitive trend of one moment may become a short chapter in a much longer account. An expandable history can absorb new media, new audiences, and changing debates without rewriting its entire structure every year.'
])])
}
arch=json.loads((ROOT/'data/architecture.json').read_text())
titles={sub['id']:sub['title'] for part in arch['parts'] for ch in part['chapters'] for sub in ch['subchapters']}
manifest=[]
for id,(kicker,description,sections) in ARTICLES.items():
    assert id in titles,id
    blocks=[]
    for i,(heading,paras) in enumerate(sections,1):
        blocks.append({'id':f'{id}:b{i:02}','title':heading,'paragraphs':paras,'label':'Prototype editorial narrative · unsourced sample','sequence':i})
    wc=sum(len(re.findall(r"\b[\w’'-]+\b",p)) for b in blocks for p in b['paragraphs'])
    article={'id':id,'title':titles[id],'kicker':kicker,'description':description,'blocks':blocks,'wordCount':wc,'status':'sample','disclosure':'Demonstration prose for the interaction prototype. No claim of source verification or complete coverage of this canonical subject.'}
    (ROOT/f'data/articles/{id}.json').write_text(json.dumps(article,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
    manifest.append({'id':id,'title':titles[id],'kicker':kicker,'description':description,'blocks':len(blocks),'wordCount':wc,'path':f'./data/articles/{id}.json'})
(ROOT/'data/content-manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps({'version':'phase-1.0','count':len(manifest),'articles':manifest,'sampleWordCount':sum(x['wordCount'] for x in manifest)},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
print('Created',len(manifest),'sample articles with',sum(x['wordCount'] for x in manifest),'words and',sum(x['blocks'] for x in manifest),'lazy-load blocks')
